#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# ratios

#% text
# `ratios(xs)` gives the successive ratios `xs[2]/xs[1]`, `xs[3]/xs[2]`, ...; `ratios(xs, k)` applies it `k` times.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `ratios(xs, [k1, k2, ...])` applies it `k1` times at level 1, `k2` times at level 2, and so on.

#% item
# Rows of a matrix are divided elementwise.

#% item
# `ratios` is the multiplicative counterpart of `differences`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
ratios([a, b, c, d, e])
#% output
#= [b/a, c/b, d/c, e/d]

#% cell
ratios([a, b, c, d, e], 2)
#% output
#= [a*c/b^2, b*d/c^2, c*e/d^2]

#% cell
ratios(map(i => 2^i, range(0, 10)))
#% output
#= [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2]

#% cell
ratios([[a, b, c], [d, e, f], [g, h, k]], [0, 1])
#% output
#= [[b/a, c/b], [e/d, f/e], [h/g, k/h]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `differences`, `fold_list`, `linear_recurrence`
